 1 big thing: Making sense of cyber mayhem 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios. Stock: Getty Images Most Americans are baffled and alarmed by the sudden explosion of technical glitches hitting air travel, banks and work products like Microsoft Outlook. The outages seem genuinely scary and related, Jim VandeHei and Mike Allen write in a "Behind the Curtain" column. 

 So what the hell is going on? The most likely answer: This is the first true wave of unintended consequences of increasingly powerful artificial intelligence hitting us at once.

 🤖 The big picture: In short, AI is exceptionally good at building software, and therefore exceptionally good at spotting and exploiting bugs in large technical, software-based systems.

 The glitches come from a mix of sources — some innocuous, some scary, including: routine updates to aging infrastructure; humans scrambling to patch vulnerabilities discovered by AI; hackers exploiting existing weaknesses; and bad actors using swarms of AI agents to launch increasingly sophisticated cyberattacks. That's the new reality that government officials and AI experts believe we're entering.

 They expect this period of disruption and heightened vulnerability to persist for months, as powerful new AI tools expose weaknesses buried in decades-old systems. The catchphrase gaining currency: "Software Beirut" — a period of cascading failures, many of our own making, that could grow worse before things get better. 🫨 What this means for YOU: Understand the different causes and resist the temptation to panic every time something goes haywire.

 It's easy to hop on social media and let the algorithm drag you into doomsday thinking. 💵 You should also prepare: Reset and protect your passwords. Keep more cash on hand if possible, in case your bank is down for a day or two. Buy some basic emergency supplies in case power or water do go out.

 Sadly, we believe this season of glitches and possible cyberattacks will persist. Better to be prepared. Go deeper . 

 
 
